        "2": {
            "name": "text_block",
            "id": 311,
            "position_x": 862,
            "position_y": 45,
            "width": 768,
            "height": 1016,
            "visibility": "show_all",
            "draggable": true,
            "presentation_state": "show_all",
            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Replaces every value with the median of its neighbourhood: the middle value when the neighbours are put in order. A single wild value - a glitch, a dropped frame, a sensor's spike - is never in the middle, so it simply disappears, while the values around it are left almost untouched.\n\nThat is what makes it the standard despiking filter. An averaging filter (scipy. gaussian_filter, np.convolve) smears a spike into its neighbours and rounds off every sharp step; the median removes the spike entirely and keeps a step a step.\n\nSYNTAX:\nscipy.median_filter <size ...>\nDefault: size 3.\n\nEXAMPLES:\nscipy.median_filter 5\nscipy.median_filter 5 1\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nThe array (a list, image or grid). Every input is filtered and sent.\n\nsize (text box, also an inlet):\nHow many values in each neighbourhood, centred on each value: 3 looks one each side. A spike up to (size - 1) / 2 samples long is removed. One number for every axis, or one per axis - 1 leaves that axis alone, so 5 1 despikes a buffer of frames x joints along time only.\n\nedges:\nAn option, under the node: what lies beyond the edges, as for scipy.gaussian_filter.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nfiltered:\nThe despiked array, the same shape.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nA wave with two glitches in it (left): 9 where it should be about 1, and -8 where it should be about -0.5. With size 3 both are gone and the rest of the wave is as it was (right). A metro re-sends the list twice a second.\n\nRELATED:\nscipy.gaussian_filter blurs instead. np.nanmedian and np.median take the median of a whole array. np.isnan and np.nan_to_num deal with gaps rather than spikes.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 1000,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Replaces every value with the median of its neighbourhood: the middle value when the neighbours are put in order. A single wild value - a glitch, a dropped frame, a sensor's spike - is never in the middle, so it simply disappears, while the values around it are left almost untouched.\n\nThat is what makes it the standard despiking filter. An averaging filter (scipy. gaussian_filter, np.convolve) smears a spike into its neighbours and rounds off every sharp step; the median removes the spike entirely and keeps a step a step.\n\nSYNTAX:\nscipy.median_filter <size ...>\nDefault: size 3.\n\nEXAMPLES:\nscipy.median_filter 5\nscipy.median_filter 5 1\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nThe array (a list, image or grid). Every input is filtered and sent.\n\nsize (text box, also an inlet):\nHow many values in each neighbourhood, centred on each value: 3 looks one each side. A spike up to (size - 1) / 2 samples long is removed. One number for every axis, or one per axis - 1 leaves that axis alone, so 5 1 despikes a buffer of frames x joints along time only.\n\nedges:\nAn option, under the node: what lies beyond the edges, as for scipy.gaussian_filter.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nfiltered:\nThe despiked array, the same shape.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nA wave with two glitches in it (left): 9 where it should be about 1, and -8 where it should be about -0.5. With size 3 both are gone and the rest of the wave is as it was (right). A metro re-sends the list twice a second.\n\nRELATED:\nscipy.gaussian_filter blurs instead. np.nanmedian and np.median take the median of a whole array. np.isnan and np.nan_to_num deal with gaps rather than spikes."
        },
